patch_description:
The only passing output, and every target_style example, is in a first-person register that reacts and judges: "I guess", "I was disappointed", "I would not steer most people away", rhetorical questions, parenthetical asides and specific nouns from the situation. The flagged outputs are all third-person explainers. They open with a topic sentence ("X is dangerous because...") and then walk through mechanism and consequence in even, polished sentences.

This edit keeps the short prompt that scored best on task quality (8.12). It adds guidance to write as a person giving their own take:
- first-person judgments and hedges;
- occasionally a question to self or a parenthetical aside;
- names taken from the task;
- no opening topic sentence that restates the question as a thesis;
- no generalized "you" explainer.

Vague experience framing is allowed but invented events are not, so facts stay safe. The edit also asks the model to vary sentence openings and to let sentences be mildly clunky, the way the target examples are ("I guess... I guess..."). Temperature goes up slightly to 0.9 to add lexical variety without derailing the task.
